WEBVTT

00:00:08.023 --> 00:00:14.714
So, I'd like to end with a summary of what
I've looked at today, and some recommendations.

00:00:14.714 --> 00:00:21.224
The figures show that sales are strongly seasonal,
and that customers depend on good information

00:00:21.224 --> 00:00:27.244
on the showroom floor. So I'd like to recommend
we concentrate on discounting in the off-seasons,

00:00:27.244 --> 00:00:32.594
and spend more time on briefing our sales
representatives.

00:00:32.594 --> 00:00:36.303
That's all I have for now. Are there any questions?

00:00:36.303 --> 00:00:43.114
You mentioned that the sales figures may also
reflect economic trends. Can you expand on

00:00:43.114 --> 00:00:44.504
that?

00:00:44.504 --> 00:00:49.684
Well, we are always going to depend on the
economy. As I understand it, we can look forward

00:00:49.684 --> 00:00:53.193
to an improvement this year. Does that answer
your question?

00:00:53.193 --> 00:00:57.423
Are you saying we're in for a period of growth?

00:00:57.423 --> 00:01:02.033
Well, I'm afraid that's a bit outside my area
of expertise, but that's what the papers are

00:01:02.033 --> 00:01:03.374
suggesting.

00:01:03.374 --> 00:01:05.594
If you believe it.

00:01:05.594 --> 00:01:11.604
You suggested that we might be spending too
much on advertising. Can you clarify that?

00:01:11.604 --> 00:01:15.124
It's hard to quantify without better data.

00:01:15.124 --> 00:01:18.403
Sorry. Could you repeat that?

00:01:18.403 --> 00:01:23.134
We don't have the figures to really know how
effective our advertising is.

00:01:23.134 --> 00:01:29.164
In my experience you can't do without advertising.

00:01:29.164 --> 00:01:31.494
Time for one last question.

00:01:31.494 --> 00:01:32.494
I have one.

00:01:32.494 --> 00:01:33.503
Yes John?

00:01:33.503 --> 00:01:34.503
Is it time for coffee?

00:01:34.503 --> 00:01:40.244
Okay, we'll wrap up now. Thank you for your
input everyone.

00:01:40.244 --> 00:01:51.554
Thank you.
Today we're looking at how to end a presentation,

00:01:51.554 --> 00:01:58.393
and how to deal with questions. Let's look
first at how Barbara concludes, or finishes

00:01:58.393 --> 00:02:02.113
her presentation.
I'd like to end with a summary of what I've

00:02:02.113 --> 00:02:08.924
looked at today, and some recommendations.
Barbara says she'd like to end with a summary,

00:02:08.924 --> 00:02:11.924
and some recommendations.

00:02:11.924 --> 00:02:17.334
When ending a presentation or a talk, you
may do this with a summary - this is a short

00:02:17.334 --> 00:02:23.114
statement of your main points - with a conclusion,
which is a result of all the information you've

00:02:23.114 --> 00:02:30.244
presented, or recommendations, which are things
you think should be done. In all cases, they

00:02:30.244 --> 00:02:35.453
should be clear and concise, or not too long.

00:02:35.453 --> 00:02:40.873
Practise with Barbara some phrases you can
use to introduce your final points.

00:02:40.873 --> 00:02:45.754
I'd like to end with some recommendations

00:02:45.754 --> 00:02:47.064
I'll finish with a summary

00:02:47.064 --> 00:02:57.494
What can we conclude from all this?
Listen to the difference between the summary

00:02:57.494 --> 00:03:02.894
and the recommendations.
The figures show that sales are strongly seasonal,

00:03:02.894 --> 00:03:08.873
and that customers depend on good information
on the showroom floor. So I'd like to recommend

00:03:08.873 --> 00:03:14.013
we concentrate on discounting in the off-seasons,
and spend more time on briefing our sales

00:03:14.013 --> 00:03:18.104
representatives.
Her summary consists of the main points from

00:03:18.104 --> 00:03:25.194
her talk about sales figures. There are two
points. Her recommendations are what she thinks

00:03:25.194 --> 00:03:31.674
the company should do in the future, and there
are two of these as well. Here are some phrases

00:03:31.674 --> 00:03:39.843
you can use to introduce a summary and recommendations.
In summary

00:03:39.843 --> 00:03:43.394
To summarise

00:03:43.394 --> 00:03:47.034
I'd like to recommend that

00:03:47.034 --> 00:03:53.873
My recommendations are
Let's look at how Barbara finishes her talk,

00:03:53.873 --> 00:03:59.354
and asks for questions.
That's all I have for now. Are there any questions?

00:03:59.354 --> 00:04:05.384
Here's Barbara with some useful phrases for
you to practise if asking for questions.

00:04:05.384 --> 00:04:09.624
Are there any questions?

00:04:09.624 --> 00:04:11.424
I'll answer any questions now.

00:04:11.424 --> 00:04:22.113
Does anyone have any questions?
At the end of a talk, you may ask for questions,

00:04:22.113 --> 00:04:28.654
or for comments, or for a general discussion.
You need to let your audience know what you

00:04:28.654 --> 00:04:34.824
want them to do, like this:
Are there any comments?

00:04:34.824 --> 00:04:39.734
I'd like to open it up for discussion.

00:04:39.734 --> 00:04:46.044
Let's look at how Denise asks a question.
You mentioned that the sales figures may also

00:04:46.044 --> 00:04:50.804
reflect economic trends. Can you expand on
that?

00:04:50.804 --> 00:04:57.554
Denise does two things. First she re-states
something Barbara said, then she asks her

00:04:57.554 --> 00:05:03.994
to expand on it, or say more about it. It's
a good idea when asking a question to state

00:05:03.994 --> 00:05:08.883
what you think the speaker said, so everyone
knows what the question is about.

00:05:08.883 --> 00:05:12.494
You might say
You mentioned that

00:05:12.494 --> 00:05:17.693
You suggested that
You stated that

00:05:17.693 --> 00:05:23.574
In the question you can ask the speaker to
respond in different ways. Denise asks her

00:05:23.574 --> 00:05:31.184
to expand, but she could also clarify, or
make clearer, or explain further. Practise

00:05:31.184 --> 00:05:38.424
some phrases you can use for this:
Can you expand on that?

00:05:38.424 --> 00:05:42.563
Can you clarify that?

00:05:42.563 --> 00:05:50.663
Could you explain that a bit further?
Listen to how Barbara answers Denise's question:

00:05:50.663 --> 00:05:55.684
Well, we are always going to depend on the
economy. As I understand it, we can look forward

00:05:55.684 --> 00:05:59.614
to an improvement this year. Does that answer
your question?

00:05:59.614 --> 00:06:06.154
In her answer Barbara uses the phrase, "As
I understand it". By using this phrase she

00:06:06.154 --> 00:06:12.104
is signalling that this is not really her
area of expertise. She is getting her information

00:06:12.104 --> 00:06:16.544
from somewhere else. You could also use phrases
like:

00:06:16.544 --> 00:06:22.514
As far as I know, or
My information is that

00:06:22.514 --> 00:06:27.943
Notice that she also checks whether Denise
is satisfied with her answer by saying:

00:06:27.943 --> 00:06:30.984
Does that answer your question?

00:06:30.984 --> 00:06:36.894
If she wanted to, Denise could ask a further
question, but Tan does it for her.

00:06:36.894 --> 00:06:40.584
Are you saying we're in for a period of growth?

00:06:40.584 --> 00:06:45.204
Well, I'm afraid that's a bit outside my area
of expertise, but that's what the papers are

00:06:45.204 --> 00:06:49.894
suggesting.
Tan is asking for clarification. He wants

00:06:49.894 --> 00:06:58.114
Barbara to say more about her point. He does
this by checking that he has understood her.

00:06:58.114 --> 00:07:03.514
Practise with Tan some phrases you can use
to ask for clarification.

00:07:03.514 --> 00:07:07.093
Are you saying

00:07:07.093 --> 00:07:11.174
So you're saying that

00:07:11.174 --> 00:07:15.934
So, are you suggesting that

00:07:15.934 --> 00:07:23.954
If I follow you, you're suggesting that
Let's look at some more questions now.

00:07:23.954 --> 00:07:29.964
You suggested that we might be spending too
much on advertising. Can you clarify that?

00:07:29.964 --> 00:07:33.484
It's hard to quantify without better data.

00:07:33.484 --> 00:07:36.764
Sorry. Could you repeat that?

00:07:36.764 --> 00:07:41.473
We don't have the figures to really know how
effective our advertising is.

00:07:41.473 --> 00:07:48.633
Notice that, as before, Denise restates what
she thinks Barbara said, by saying, "You suggested

00:07:48.633 --> 00:07:54.954
that," then asks her to clarify, by saying,
"Can you clarify that?"

00:07:54.954 --> 00:08:01.924
and Tan asks her to repeat something simply
by asking, "Could you repeat that?" Notice

00:08:01.924 --> 00:08:09.313
too that when Barbara answers Tan, she uses
different words. If someone asks you to repeat,

00:08:09.313 --> 00:08:16.424
or clarify, it's better to rephrase, than
simply say the same thing again. Notice how

00:08:16.424 --> 00:08:24.424
Denise puts in her own comment.
In my experience you can't do without advertising.

00:08:24.424 --> 00:08:31.413
This is Denise's comment, or opinion, not
a question. She shows this by starting, "in

00:08:31.413 --> 00:08:40.514
my experience". She could also have said,
"in my opinion" or "as I understand it".

00:08:40.514 --> 00:08:45.443
Let's see now how Barbara finishes her presentation.
Time for one last question.

00:08:45.443 --> 00:08:47.464
I have one.

00:08:47.464 --> 00:08:48.504
Yes John?

00:08:48.504 --> 00:08:49.504
Is it time for coffee?

00:08:49.504 --> 00:08:54.544
Okay, we'll wrap up now. Thank you for your
input everyone.

00:08:54.544 --> 00:08:59.894
Thank you.
The person giving the talk is in control,

00:08:59.894 --> 00:09:06.604
so she needs to signal that she is finished.
She does this by saying, "Time for one last

00:09:06.604 --> 00:09:08.404
question."

00:09:08.404 --> 00:09:11.933
Practise some phrases you could use to end
a presentation.

00:09:11.933 --> 00:09:16.293
Time for one last question

00:09:16.293 --> 00:09:19.833
Are there any more questions?

00:09:19.833 --> 00:09:27.974
I think we'll wrap it up now
So remember, after a talk, give a summary

00:09:27.974 --> 00:09:35.514
and perhaps some recommendations or conclusions.
Ask for questions or comments. When asking

00:09:35.514 --> 00:09:41.214
questions, state what you understood from
the speaker first, and then say what you want

00:09:41.214 --> 00:09:50.524
them to do - clarify, or explain, or expand.
Well, that about wraps us up for today. Thanks

00:09:50.524 --> 00:09:54.183
for listening, and I'll see you next time
on The Business of English.